---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import ProjectGrid from '../components/ProjectGrid.astro';
import { projects } from '../data/projects';
---

<Layout>
	<Nav />

	<main class="mx-auto max-w-[960px] px-5">
		{/* Hero. The masthead above already carries the name at full size, so this
		    says what the name does and hands off to About — the page's only route
		    there since the nav links came out (2026-08-01). Held to --measure so a
		    wide viewport can't run the line out past a readable length.

		    It used to be the about page's opening line verbatim, matched to its
		    type size so arriving from either direction read as one voice. That
		    stopped being true when this line became its own sentence: the two
		    pages now open differently on purpose, the home page saying what the
		    work IS and About saying who does it. The size match went with it —
		    about-page had followed About's 08-02 step down to the base 19px on
		    THIS line too, and that change was dropped when the two branches were
		    merged, since there is no longer a shared sentence to keep in step.
		    If the two are ever written the same again, re-match the size. */}
		{/* Centred, both lines, each on its own axis (2026-08-02, Hiro). This
		    REPLACES the shared-left-edge lockup from earlier the same day, and the
		    reason it can: that arrangement existed because the second line was a
		    bare text link that had to read as hanging off the sentence, which a
		    second centring axis would have broken. It's a button now — a boxed
		    control is self-evidently its own object, so it doesn't need the
		    sentence's left edge to say so, and the hero reads as one deliberate
		    centred block instead. */}
		{/* Padding stepped up one full stop 2026-08-03 (Hiro: let the hero breathe
		    more) — 64/80 → 96/112, and 80/96 → 112/128 from sm up. Stepped again
		    2026-08-05, same ask and the same way: 112/128, and 128/144 from sm up.
		    The 1rem top/bottom ASYMMETRY is deliberate and survives both
		    increases: the space below has to separate the hero from the projects
		    grid, while the space above only has to clear the nav bar, which is
		    already a ruled edge. Keep stepping BOTH values together if it goes
		    up again — the gap is the point, not the individual numbers. */}
		{/* The hero reveals as ONE block (2026-08-06, Hiro) — the sentence and its
		    button enter together, --i 0 of a two-beat cascade (the projects grid is
		    --i 1). They were two entries (--i 0 and 1, with the cards at +2) until
		    the per-element stagger was flagged against the migaki carve-out —
		    "one entrance on the hero, everything else static" — and collapsed:
		    elements inside a block enter WITH it, same rule the hairline finale
		    already enforces for the card dividers. */}
		<section class="reveal pt-28 pb-32 text-center sm:pt-32 sm:pb-36" style="--i: 0">
			{/* Still capped at --measure so a wide viewport can't run the line out
			    past a readable length; mx-auto centres the box the cap creates. */}
			{/* --ink-hero, a step PAST the body ink away from the paper (2026-08-03,
			    Hiro: this line and the button should contrast more, closer to
			    white). It briefly went the other way, to --ink-soft, on a reading
			    of "lighter" as quieter — wrong, and the correction is worth
			    keeping: in the dark theme this line already sat near the top of
			    the range, so "lighter" could only have meant brighter.
			    This makes the sentence the highest-contrast text on the site,
			    ahead of body ink. That is the point — it is the page's opening
			    statement and the only thing above the rule — but it does mean the
			    hero now out-contrasts the wordmark, so if the mark ever needs to
			    lead again, it is this token it has to answer to. See --ink-hero in
			    global.css for why the two themes are proportional rather than
			    inverse. */}
			{/* Inherits the body's 300. It briefly carried font-normal: the
			    2026-08-04 APCA audit put this line 0.125px under the floor in the
			    LIGHT theme (Lc 88.5 wants ≥21.5px at w300; it is 21.375px), so 400
			    was the minimum that cleared. Hiro asked for it lighter the same
			    day, knowing that, so 300 stands and this is a deliberate miss, not
			    an oversight — don't "fix" it back to 400 on sight.
			    Worth knowing if it ever gets revisited: the shortfall is 0.125px,
			    so SIZE clears it as easily as weight — text-[1.15rem] (21.85px)
			    would pass at 300 and keep the lighter drawing. Dark was never the
			    problem (Lc 93.5, wants 20px at w300); this is a light-theme-only
			    margin, and it moves with --ink-hero. */}
			<p class="mx-auto max-w-[var(--measure)] text-lg leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-hero)]">
				Design engineering, with good sense and taste.
			</p>
			{/* The page's only route to /about, so it's the site's one real button.
			    mt-6 rather than the old link's mt-4 — a control needs more air off
			    the line above it than a word set in the same size did.

			    The arrow is DRAWN AT REST and doesn't animate, unlike the project
			    cards' (which appear on hover). Different job: on a card the arrow
			    is a flourish on something already obviously clickable — bordered
			    image, whole-card cursor, a panel that lights up. Here it's the only
			    thing distinguishing a button that navigates from a button that
			    performs an action, and that's information you want before you
			    hover, not after. .btn's flex + gap owns the spacing, so the glyph
			    needs no margin of its own. */}
			<p class="mt-6">
				<a href="/about" class="btn">
					About Hiro Eernisse
					<svg
						viewBox="0 0 24 24"
						fill="none"
						stroke="currentColor"
						stroke-width="2"
						stroke-linecap="round"
						stroke-linejoin="round"
						aria-hidden="true"
						class="inline-block h-[0.85rem] w-[0.85rem]"
					>
						<line x1="4" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"></line>
						<polyline points="12 5 19 12 12 19"></polyline>
					</svg>
				</a>
			</p>
		</section>

		{/* Projects */}
		{/* The second of the page's two hairlines (2026-08-03, Hiro), the first
		    being the nav's own bottom edge. Drawn in global.css (#projects::before)
		    off the shared --rule gradient, which fades both of them out at their
		    ends — so they read as one device: the page is indexed into a bar, an
		    opening statement and a body of work.

		    This one is held to the CONTENT column rather than run full-bleed like
		    the nav's. Different jobs — the nav's line is the underside of a bar
		    that spans the viewport, while this one is a break inside the column,
		    and it lands on the same left edge as the leftmost card's year, the
		    wordmark above it and the copyright below. A full-bleed rule here would
		    read as a second bar rather than as a division of the text block.

		    The space is deliberately lopsided: the hero keeps its full breathing
		    room above the line, and the list opens at pt-12 below it, so the rule
		    belongs to the section it introduces rather than floating between two
		    equal gaps. */}
		<section id="projects" class="scroll-mt-20 pt-12 pb-16 sm:pt-14">
			{/* The grid, the travelling hover panel and the script that drives it
			    all moved into ProjectGrid.astro on 2026-08-10, when the project
			    pages grew a "More projects" strip and needed the same three things.
			    Nothing changed in the move — the rendered markup here is identical
			    — so every comment that used to live at this spot now lives in that
			    component, next to the code it describes.

			    What stays a decision of THIS page: .reveal and --i 1. The grid is
			    the second of the home page's two blocks (the hero is --i 0), and
			    the reveal sits on #project-list rather than on #projects so the
			    section's rule (#projects::before) stays OUTSIDE it — page-level
			    structure enters in the hairline finale, not with the content. See
			    the finale block in global.css, and the Props note in the component
			    for why the caller owns this rather than the grid. */}
			<ProjectGrid projects={projects} class="reveal" style="--i: 1" />
		</section>
	</main>

	{/* text-xs, down from text-sm (2026-08-03, Hiro). A copyright line is the
	    lowest-priority text on the page, and it was sharing a size with real
	    body copy. The projects grid stepped down in the same pass. */}
	<footer class="mx-auto max-w-[960px] px-5 py-10 text-xs text-[var(--ink-soft)]">
		<span>© 2026 Hiro Eernisse</span>
	</footer>

</Layout>
